33,2,1,talk,Minato,,"It seems some people are doing a lot of work by hand, and some projects were left without anyone to lead them. I worry that some of our friends might be too tired.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings are concerning. Network coverage at customer plants is unreliable, and the approval process for visitor devices is incredibly slow. We are relying on conditions we cannot control.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,1,talk,George,,"The capital budget is frozen, though we have some room in the software allowance. However, I've found that per-head licensing costs are climbing, while fixed-fee work remains stable. We must be mindful of long-term costs.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,May,,"It's a bit of a tragedy, really! My research shows that the big, ambitious projects requiring everyone's daily input just wither away. Meanwhile, the small, niche tools are the only ones still breathing.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've noticed a worrying pattern in the incident logs. We have misrouted deliveries caused by conflicting address records, and our in-house developers are completely tied up until the third quarter. We are lacking both data consistency and manpower.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Midori, it sounds like the developers are very busy. If we pick a project that needs them, will they be okay? I don't want anyone to feel overwhelmed.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Midori, that is a valid concern. Combined with my findings on the slow device approvals and poor network coverage at customer sites, any project requiring both developers and on-site access seems quite risky.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,George,,"@Jonathan, I agree. We should also be cautious about the pricing model. If we choose a solution with per-head licensing, the costs will only escalate as we scale, which is risky given the frozen capital budget.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,May,,"@George, you're so focused on the pennies! But look at the bigger picture: we're wasting an hour a day just copying records. We need something that actually sticks, not another ""grand vision"" that dies in a month.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Midori,,"@May, I completely agree. It's the small, daily frustrations—like those misrouted deliveries I found—that really drain our energy. We should prioritize a solution that fixes these everyday losses rather than something that just sounds impressive.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Minato,,"@May, copying records every day sounds very tiring. I hope we can find a way to make the work easier for everyone so they can have more time to rest.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minato, I agree. Peace of mind comes from a manageable workload. If we can avoid projects that depend on unstable external networks or slow vendor approvals, we can ensure a much smoother experience for everyone involved.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,George,,"@May, it is not about pennies, but about sustainability. If we ignore the renewal costs and the frozen capital line, we are simply delaying a crisis for next year. We need a stable, fixed-cost solution.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,May,,"@George, sustainability is exactly why we should stop chasing ghosts! If we pick a ""stable"" project that nobody actually uses because it's too demanding, that's the real waste of money. Let's be realistic for once.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@George, @May, please let's try to find a middle ground. We need a project that is financially sustainable, yes, but it must also solve the real, grinding problems our staff face every day without adding to their burden.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""George"",""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I agree with Midori. I hope we can choose something that helps the people who are already working so hard, without making them do even more work. It would be nice if everyone felt supported.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I believe we are in agreement. We should seek a project that is low-impact on our developers, avoids the instability of customer-site access, and provides a practical, fixed-cost benefit to our daily operations.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,George,,I concur. A project that avoids per-head licensing and relies on fixed-fee work will be the only way to ensure we don't face a budget crisis during next year's renewals. Let us see the candidates.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,May,,"I'm just glad we're finally on the same page! Let's hope the candidates actually offer something practical and niche, rather than another fancy disaster waiting to happen. I'm ready to see the list!","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I am ready as well. I hope we find a proposal that truly values the effort of our staff by solving those small, persistent errors that make their days so difficult.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
